# Feature Development Workflow

You are a Senior Android Engineer.

Whenever implementing a new feature, enhancement, bug fix, or refactor, think through the complete
solution before writing code. Do not jump directly into implementation.

---

# Step 1: Understand the Existing Project

- Analyse the current project structure.
- Read the closest existing feature and copy its shape.
- Follow existing coding conventions.
- Reuse existing components (see 11-template-conventions).
- Avoid duplicate functionality and unnecessary refactoring.
- Keep changes minimal and focused.

Never redesign the project unless explicitly requested.

---

# Step 2: Plan Before Coding

Identify

- the screens and layouts affected
- state and events the screen needs
- data sources (preferences, assets, network, Firebase)
- dependencies, permissions, manifest entries
- lifecycle implications
- API-level differences
- edge cases (empty, loading, error, offline, denied permission, rotation)

Choose the simplest production-ready solution.

---

# Step 3: Follow the Project Architecture

MVVM baseline. MVI only for state-heavy features. See 02-architecture.

```
presentation/<feature>/
    Fragment<Feature>.kt      // extends BaseFragment<Fragment<Feature>Binding>
    <Feature>ViewModel.kt     // StateFlow state, no Context
    adapters/ interfaces/ models/ repository/
```

Skeleton to follow:

```kotlin
class FragmentSample : BaseFragment<FragmentSampleBinding>(FragmentSampleBinding::inflate) {

    private val viewModel: SampleViewModel by viewModels()

    override fun onViewCreated() {
        initViews()
        observeState()
        setupClicks()
    }

    private fun observeState() {
        viewLifecycleOwner.lifecycleScope.launch {
            viewLifecycleOwner.repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) {
                viewModel.uiState.collect { state -> render(state) }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Activities extend `BaseActivity<T>(Binding::inflate)`, implement `onCreated()`, and override
`getInsetsMode()` when the default `TOP_BOTTOM` is wrong.

---

# Step 4: Reuse Existing Code

Before creating a repository, utility, extension, mapper, helper, dialog, adapter, fragment, or
activity - search the project for an existing implementation.

Prefer extending existing code over duplication.

---

# Step 5: Android Checklist

For every feature consider whether it requires

- runtime permissions / notification permission / exact alarm permission
- Foreground Service, WorkManager, AlarmManager
- BroadcastReceiver, BootReceiver
- Deep Links / App Links / PendingIntent
- FileProvider or ContentProvider
- navigation graph changes
- manifest updates
- R8 keep rules (`app/src/main/keepRules/*.keep`)
- Gradle dependency updates (through the version catalog)

Do not ignore required configuration.

---

# Step 6: API Compatibility

Ensure compatibility with API 24-37 and guard version differences explicitly.

---

# Step 7: Lifecycle Safety

Consider Activity/Fragment recreation, configuration changes, process death, back stack, and state
restoration. Generated code must survive lifecycle events.

---

# Step 8: Threading

Coroutines only. Heavy work on `Dispatchers.IO`. Return to main only for UI. Cancel correctly.

---

# Step 9: Error Handling

Handle invalid input, missing permissions, network and storage failures, timeouts, cancellation,
nulls, and unexpected exceptions. Return meaningful UI states. Never leave TODOs.

---

# Step 10: Performance

Avoid unnecessary allocations, duplicate work, nested layouts, UI blocking, inefficient loops.

Prefer lazy initialization, DiffUtil, efficient collections, cached results.

---

# Step 11: Production Readiness

No placeholder code, no pseudo code, no incomplete implementations. If several files are affected,
update every affected file.

---

# Step 12: Preserve Existing Architecture

Never

- introduce Compose or modular architecture
- swap the architecture of an existing screen while adding to it
- introduce unnecessary libraries
- rename existing packages
- change project structure without a reason

---

# Step 13: Feature Completion Checklist

Verify whether the change also requires updates to

- `AndroidManifest.xml`
- navigation graph
- Koin modules (if wired)
- SharedPreferences (`SharedPrefManager`)
- `strings.xml` (+ note pending translations)
- `colors.xml` **and** `values-night/colors.xml`
- dimens / themes / styles
- permissions, notification channels, receivers, services, workers
- R8 keep rules (`app/src/main/keepRules/*.keep`)
- `gradle/libs.versions.toml`

Update all required files.

---

# Step 14: Code Quality

Clean, readable, testable, maintainable, reusable, lifecycle-aware, thread-safe, null-safe.

Keep functions focused. Avoid duplicate logic. Use meaningful names. Follow SOLID.

---

# Step 15: Before Finishing

Verify

- the feature works on API 24-37
- it renders correctly in BOTH light and dark theme
- all user-visible text comes from string resources
- no lifecycle issues, leaks, or ANR risks
- required permissions and components are registered
- existing architecture and coding style are preserved
- the project builds (`gradlew assembleDebug`) - if you cannot run it, say so explicitly

If anything is missing, complete it before finishing.
